      "content": "Cairo–Asmara Axis Root of Security Crisis in Ethiopia, Says Analyst Prof. Al Mariam - ENA English - ENA Skip to Content Cairo&#8211;Asmara Axis Root of Security Crisis in Ethiopia, Says Analyst Prof. Al Mariam - ENA English Live: አማርኛ ትግርኛ Afaan Oromoo Af‑Soomaali Qafar Afa English Français عربي Politics Social Economy Sport Technology Environment Feature Videos About Us Cairo–Asmara Axis Root of Security Crisis in Ethiopia, Says Analyst Prof. Al Mariam 🔇Unmute Addis Ababa, October 24, 2026 (ENA) — The ongoing security threats and regional instability facing Ethiopia stem from a long-standing strategic alignment between Cairo and Asmara to cripple the nation’s development, according to the political analyst Al Mariam. In an article published on Megatopia, Professor Al Mariam analyzed historical remarks made by Eritrean President Isaias Afewerki during a press conference in the early 1990s, noting that Egypt and Eritrea have historically coordinated to deprive Ethiopia of peace and generate continuous internal hardship. Al Mariam said the underlying strategic objective binding the axis is securing Egyptian dominance over the Nile waters. He explained that Egyptian political strategy relies on keeping upstream Nile Basin nations in a state of perpetual turmoil to restrict their ability to carry out vital riverine development projects along the Abay River. According to the analyst, President Isaias’ explicit acknowledgment of the campaign to \"give Ethiopia work\" reflects a deliberate strategy of exhaustion. By fomenting internal conflicts, funding armed insurgent groups, and conducting cross-border military incursions, the axis aims to force the Ethiopian government into continuous defense spending. This systematic diversion of national resources away from socio-economic and infrastructure projects serves to weaken state institutions and undermine national unity, the analyst elaborated. Al Mariam emphasized that the Eritrean regime effectively functions as a client state and proxy for Cairo, carrying out actions that compromise the stability of the Horn of Africa and the Red Sea corridor. He added that the calculated acts of destabilization orchestrated by Egypt and Eritrea constitute a grave threat not only to Ethiopia's sovereignty but also to the broader peace and security of the entire region. © Ethiopian News Agency 2023 Hidden",
      "summary": "Political analyst Prof. Al Mariam has attributed Ethiopia's ongoing security crisis to a strategic alliance between Cairo and Asmara, aimed at undermining the nation's development. In his analysis published on Megatopia, he references historical comments made by Eritrean President Isaias Afewerki, highlighting a long-standing collaboration between Egypt and Eritrea to destabilize Ethiopia. Al Mariam argues that the primary goal of this alliance is to maintain Egyptian control over the Nile waters, with Egypt's strategy focused on keeping upstream nations in turmoil to hinder their development projects. He points out that Afewerki's acknowledgment of a campaign to \"give Ethiopia work\" reflects a deliberate strategy to exhaust the Ethiopian government. This includes fostering internal conflicts, funding insurgent groups, and conducting military incursions, which divert resources from essential socio-economic initiatives. Al Mariam warns that these destabilizing actions threaten not only Ethiopia's sovereignty but also the broader peace and security of the Horn of Africa and the Red Sea region.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/cairo-asmara-axis-root-of-security-crisis-in-ethiopia-says-a-cee43491ac.html"
    },
    {
      "title": "Ethiopia’s Strategic Interests, Historic Ties Bind It to Red Sea: Ambassador Zerihun - ENA English",
      "link": "https://www.ena.et/web/eng/w/eng_9802760",
      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:46:39 GMT",
      "image": "https://www.ena.et/documents/42142/0/861871146_965552779470377_5813143101041189492_n+%281%29.jpg/54b9c4d9-f85a-643b-fecd-71f9b851bf84?version=1.0&amp;t=1792826582424&amp;download=true",
      "content": "Ethiopia’s Strategic Interests, Historic Ties Bind It to Red Sea: Ambassador Zerihun - ENA English - ENA Skip to Content Ethiopia’s Strategic Interests, Historic Ties Bind It to Red Sea: Ambassador Zerihun - ENA English Live: አማርኛ ትግርኛ Afaan Oromoo Af‑Soomaali Qafar Afa English Français عربي Politics Social Economy Sport Technology Environment Feature Videos About Us Ethiopia’s Strategic Interests, Historic Ties Bind It to Red Sea: Ambassador Zerihun 🔇Unmute Addis Ababa, October 24, 2026 (ENA) —Ethiopia’s strategic importance, historical ties, and geographic realities make its connection to the Red Sea inseparable from its national interests, Director General for African Affairs at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Ambassador Zerihun Abebe said. Briefing journalists on the sidelines of the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa in Bahir Dar, Ambassador Zerihun underscored that Ethiopia’s pursuit of sea access is rooted in history, geography, and national security. “We have repeatedly stated that the issue of a sea outlet is a matter of survival for Ethiopia,” he said, adding that the country has developed policy strategies to advance this objective. He stressed that Ethiopia cannot be excluded from discussions on the Red Sea, given its strategic position, security interests, and role in the wider region. The evolving regional realities, he added, call for cooperation that takes Ethiopia’s interests into account. Ambassador Zerihun reaffirmed Ethiopia’s commitment to working with regional countries to advance peace, development, and shared security, describing the Tana Forum as an important platform for strengthening cooperation and addressing common challenges. He also emphasized the need for a regional anchor capable of contributing to peace, development, and stability, asserting that Ethiopia has historically played—and continues to play—such a role. The remarks come as regional and international stakeholders gather in Bahir Dar for the Tana Forum’s Special Envoys’ Dialogue, which is examining the evolving geopolitics of the Horn of Africa and mounting security challenges in the Red Sea. In his welcoming remarks, National Intelligence and Security Service (NISS), Director General Redwan Hussein highlighted the strategic importance of the Red Sea and its maritime trade routes, warning that growing militarization, competition among external powers, and attacks on commercial shipping in the Bab el-Mandeb Strait pose risks to regional stability. He emphasized that securing the Red Sea requires collective action by regional states and reliable international partners. He noted that Ethiopia has significant stake in maritime security as a country dependent on the Red Sea for its international trade. High-level discussions under the theme: “Regional Security in the Horn of Africa and the Red Sea” brought together African and international envoys to examine the region’s changing security and geopolitical landscape. The 12th Tana High-Level Forum is being held under the theme “Pathways to Peace in Africa: Economic Sovereignty and Geopolitical Transformation,” bringing together policymakers, diplomats, and other stakeholders to explore African-led responses to the continent’s peace and security challenges. © Ethiopian News Agency 2023 Hidden",
      "summary": "Ethiopia's connection to the Red Sea is crucial for its national interests, as emphasized by Ambassador Zerihun Abebe during the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa. He highlighted that Ethiopia's pursuit of sea access is deeply rooted in its historical, geographical, and security contexts, asserting that having a sea outlet is vital for the country's survival. The ambassador noted that Ethiopia's strategic position and security interests necessitate its inclusion in discussions regarding the Red Sea. He called for regional cooperation that acknowledges Ethiopia's stakes, especially in light of evolving geopolitical dynamics. The forum also addressed the growing militarization and competition in the Red Sea, which pose risks to regional stability. National Intelligence and Security Service Director General Redwan Hussein stressed the importance of collective action among regional states to secure maritime routes. The Tana Forum serves as a platform for policymakers and diplomats to explore African-led solutions to peace and security challenges in the region.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/ethiopia-s-strategic-interests-historic-ties-bind-it-to-red--6004ce3ec7.html"
