      "image": "https://media.openthemagazine.com/openthemagazine/2026-10-25/la3phtv0/Donald-Trump-4.jpg?w=1200&ar=40:21&auto=format,compress&ogImage=true&mode=crop&enlarge=true&overlay=false&overlay_position=bottom&overlay_width=100",
      "content": "Trump Revives 'Shithole Countries' Controversy, Targets Senator Over Haiti Immigration Bill Logo Go Ad-Free Home India World Politics Sports Business Columns Entertainment Art & Culture Lifestyle Books Technology Open Avenues Archives Movies Cover Stories Features Health Digital Stories Logo Home India World Politics Entertainment Sports Logo NewsWrap Home World \"Shithouse Countries\": Trump Slams Senator Over Haiti Bill, Cites Immigration from Libya, Somalia \"Shithouse Countries\": Trump Slams Senator Over Haiti Bill, Cites Immigration from Libya, Somalia Open Bureau Last Updated: October 25, 2026, 16:18 IST Summarise Share Share Trump accused Ossoff of backing a bill that would bring migrants from what he described as “terror-infested countries” Trump also made disparaging claims about Somalis living in the US, alleging that 98 per cent did not work and benefited from theft. US President Donald Trump on Saturday (local time) targeted Democratic Senator Jon Ossoff over his support for legislation seeking temporary protected status for Haiti, reviving his controversial “shithole countries” remarks at a campaign rally in Perry, Georgia. Addressing a gathering in support of Republican candidates, Trump accused Ossoff of backing a bill that would bring migrants from what he described as “terror-infested countries”, naming Libya, Yemen, Haiti and Somalia. “Remember what I called them? Shithouse countries,” Trump said, recalling the backlash he faced after making similar remarks in 2018. Sign up for Open Magazine's ad-free experience Enjoy uninterrupted access to premium content and insights. Go Ad-Free Trump also made disparaging claims about Somalis living in the US, alleging that 98 per cent did not work and benefited from theft. He claimed that they lived better than Americans despite what he described as their low intelligence, making further derogatory comments about the community. Recalling an earlier meeting with Democratic senators on immigration policy, Trump said he had questioned why the US accepted immigrants from countries he considered undesirable instead of countries such as Norway, Sweden and Denmark. Open Magazine Latest Edition is Out Now! Losing Memory 16 Oct 2026 - Vol 05 | Issue 42 The making of a medical and social emergency Read Now Losing Memory The legislation at the centre of the latest controversy, S. 4814, was introduced in the US Senate on June 17 by Democratic Senator Ed Markey and other lawmakers. It sought to require the Secretary of Homeland Security to designate Haiti for temporary protected status until three months after January 20, 2029. Ossoff supported the bill by signing on as a co-sponsor. Recommended Stories US: 11 Injured in Florida A&M University Shooting, Including Six Juveniles “The French Riots Weren’t Islamist, But They Will Help Marine Le Pen To Come To Power” Strike Iran ‘Maybe Tomorrow’ or Reach a ‘Big, Beautiful’ Deal? Trump Keeps Both Options Open ‘Saddam Hussein Opened a Cheesecake Factory’: Obama Mocks Trump’s White House Makeover Cost of Living, Pensions & Housing Fuel Protests Across Europe Trump’s remarks revive a controversy dating back to January 2018, when reports emerged that he had used the phrase “shithole countries” while discussing immigration with senators behind closed doors. The meeting reportedly focused on immigration policy and the status of migrants from Haiti and African countries. According to a CNN report published in December 2025, Trump later acknowledged using the phrase during a speech in Pennsylvania, recalling his preference for immigrants from countries such as Norway, Sweden and Denmark. The report said his account matched earlier media reports about the 2018 meeting. During the original controversy, Democratic Senator Dick Durbin publicly confirmed that Trump had used the phrase. Trump denied Durbin’s account at the time, while Republican Senator David Perdue, who attended the meeting, said he did not recall Trump using the language. Then-Homeland Security Secretary Kirstjen Nielsen also said she did not remember the specific phrase. Durbin later said on the Senate floor that Trump’s acknowledgement vindicated his account of the meeting, according to CNN. The latest remarks have once again drawn attention to Trump’s rhetoric on immigration as he campaigns for Republican candidates ahead of the US midterm elections in November. With inputs from ANI Home World \"Shithouse Countries\": Trump Slams Senator Over Haiti Bill, Cites Immigration from Libya, Somalia Logo Follow us Published by the RP-Sanjiv Goenka Group, Open Magazine offers sharp commentary, in-depth reportage, and diverse perspectives on politics, culture, society, and the world of ideas. Open Media Authors Columns Subscriptions Open Magazine The RSS Century Transition in Nepal: Now What? Modi at 75 Won’t Give In What's Trending India World Entertainment Sports Reviews & More Art & Culture Movie Reviews Book Reviews Privacy and Cookie Policy About Us Media Kit Contact Us © 2026 Open Magazine. All Rights Reserved.",
      "summary": "At a campaign rally in Perry, Georgia, former President Donald Trump criticized Democratic Senator Jon Ossoff for supporting a bill that would grant temporary protected status to Haitian migrants. Trump revived his controversial \"shithole countries\" remarks, labeling Haiti, Libya, Yemen, and Somalia as \"terror-infested countries.\" He made derogatory claims about Somali immigrants in the U.S., alleging that 98% do not work and benefit from theft, while asserting they live better than Americans. Trump's comments echo his past statements from 2018, where he expressed a preference for immigrants from countries like Norway over those from Africa. The legislation, S. 4814, was introduced by Senator Ed Markey and aims to protect Haitian migrants until January 2029. Trump's rhetoric has reignited discussions on immigration as he campaigns for Republican candidates ahead of the upcoming midterm elections.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/shithouse-countries-trump-slams-senator-over-haiti-bill-cite-469494ecf1.html"
