      "title": "Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process - FTL Somalia",
      "link": "https://www.ftlsomalia.com/puntlands-deni-rejects-federal-election-process/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:56:15 GMT",
      "image": "https://ftlsomalia.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/PS-HSM-Nugaal-Elder.webp",
      "content": "Puntland's Deni Rejects Federal Election Process - Somali News today Live Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Generic selectors Exact matches only Search in title Search in content Post Type Selectors Politics Puntland Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process October 25, 2026 5 min read GAROWE , Puntland President Said Abdullahi Deni has issued a stark warning about Somalia’s political trajectory, accusing President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud of refusing an agreed-upon election and obstructing dialogue, while calling on all Somali stakeholders to convene urgently to prevent further deterioration. Speaking at the opening of the 59th session of Puntland’s parliament in Garowe on Saturday, Deni said his administration would not participate in the federal government-led electoral process, deepening the standoff over Somalia’s electoral framework. He called on Somalis to engage in dialogue and reach a political understanding on the country’s direction, stressing the need for an inclusive settlement that accounts for all stakeholders’ positions. “The Puntland government calls on all different parts of Somalia to come together urgently, to save Somali statehood,” Deni said. He also urged the international community to closely monitor Somalia’s political situation and support a consultative process to address the divisions. The Puntland leader accused President Mohamud of refusing to hold an agreed election and of obstructing talks on the electoral process. His remarks come as the federal government maintains that presidential elections will be held on May 15, 2027, with parliamentary elections set for April 15 of the same year. A Fractured Political Landscape Deni’s warning is the latest escalation in a prolonged dispute that has seen Puntland, Jubaland, and the opposition Somali Future Council reject the federal government’s electoral timetable and constitutional amendments. The opposition coalition has accused President Mohamud of taking unilateral decisions that threaten national unity and the federal system. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the political crisis , former Puntland President Abdirahman Mohamed Faroole has declared that there is “no legitimate government in Mogadishu,” reflecting the depth of the rift between Garowe and the federal capital. Puntland has formally rejected the constitutional amendments, arguing that they undermine the federal system and concentrate excessive power in the hands of the federal presidency, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of Puntland’s rejection . The political confrontation has been compounded by practical tensions. The federal government’s enforcement of the National ID requirement for domestic travel has created disruptions for Puntland residents, as NIRA does not operate registration offices in the region. Puntland has rejected the federal ID system, with Deni signing a decree in 2025 creating the Puntland Identification Authority to issue cards independently, as detailed in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the ID dispute . Hassan Sheikh Meets Puntland Elder Over Nugaal Killings Against this backdrop of political confrontation, President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud met with Puntland traditional leader Islaan Ciise Islaan Mohamed at the Presidential Palace, with discussions reportedly focusing on the deaths of 13 people along the Garmaal and Jiifle coastal areas in the Nugaal region. According to information obtained about the meeting, Islaan Ciise urged the federal government to pursue accountability, ensure justice for the victims, and protect the rights of their bereaved families. The incident has heightened concerns over civilian safety along Puntland’s coastline, particularly among fishing communities whose livelihoods depend on access to the sea. Puntland’s administration has previously said the victims were civilian fishermen and accused Turkish forces of carrying out an attack that killed people not involved in fighting. The federal government has offered a different account, with State Minister for Defence Omar Ali Abdi stating that the government was aware of military operations along the coast and that any operation against piracy would legally require federal authorization. The circumstances surrounding the strike remain contested. As reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the airstrikes controversy , Turkey has stated it has no information regarding airstrikes off the Puntland coast that killed nine people, amid growing controversy over attacks that reportedly targeted a mix of fishermen and individuals suspected of being part of pirate networks. Puntland later called for an independent international investigation into the strikes, as reported in FTL Somalia’s coverage of the demand . Puntland President Deni has addressed the escalating security situation, expressing dissatisfaction with how the strikes were conducted and emphasizing the need for legal accountability. “We are not satisfied with how things happened, and we are deeply concerned. If someone commits a crime, we do not need to take extrajudicial action; crime has a legal process to follow,” Deni stated. Critical Note Deni’s call for urgent dialogue and his decision to boycott the federal electoral process are not isolated gestures. They represent a calculated strategy by Puntland to position itself as the principal alternative center of political authority in Somalia, at a moment when the federal government’s legitimacy is contested by multiple stakeholders. The meeting between President Mohamud and Islaan Ciise is a recognition that the Nugaal killings have created a humanitarian and political crisis that cannot be ignored. But a single meeting with a traditional elder does not resolve the underlying questions about accountability, civilian protection, and the role of foreign military operations in Puntland’s territory. The families of the victims have been promised justice. Whether they receive it will depend on whether the federal government is willing to investigate an operation that Puntland believes was conducted by its Turkish partners. The two developments, Deni’s political offensive and the Nugaal killings, are connected by more than timing. They both expose the fragility of the federal compact and the extent to which Puntland’s relationship with Mogadishu has deteriorated. Deni’s warning that Somali statehood is at risk is not hyperbole. It is a description of a political landscape in which the federal government and its most powerful regional ally are moving in opposite directions, with no agreed framework for resolving their differences. The call for urgent talks is necessary. But talks require willing partners, and the gap between Garowe and Mogadishu has never been wider. Recommended Reading On ftlsomalia.com Former Puntland President Faroole: No Legitimate Government in Mogadishu Puntland Condemns Airstrikes as Turkey Denies Involvement Puntland Calls for Independent International Investigation into Deadly Airstrikes Puntland Rejects Constitutional Amendments, Says Process Lacked Consensus Puntland Rejects Federal National ID Mandate for Air Travel Facebook X Email WhatsApp LinkedIn Somali Ministers Call for Dialogue as Somaliland Elders Attend Mogadishu Event You may also like Politics Somali Ministers Call for Dialogue as Somaliland Elders Attend Mogadishu Event October 23, 2026 4 min read Politics Somali PM Hails Women’s Role in Nation-Building at Islamic Scholars Conference October 22, 2026 5 min read Politics Ambassador Máthé Meets Speaker Abdi Hashi: EU Commits to Federal and State Development Projects in Somalia October 21, 2026 4 min read Latest News BECO, Mogadishu Power and Blue Sky Announce Rate Increase Amid 70% Fuel Spike Turkish Space Agency Delegation Visits Mogadishu and Warsheikh as Defence Cooperation Expands Puntland’s Deni Rejects Federal Election Process Somali U-17 Squad Celebrated at Parliament as Calls for Sports Investment Grow Hirshabelle Victory Celebrated in Mogadishu as Political Consensus Remains Elusive Oman’s Embassy Reopens in Mogadishu as Al-Umairi Presents Credentials Abiy Ahmed Calls Sea Access an Existential Issue for Ethiopia’s 130 Million People Somalia’s Danab Commandos Launch Operation Banooda, Kill Seven Al-Shabaab Militants Federal and Regional Leaders Congratulate Guudlaawe on Hirshabelle Re-election Mogadishu Hosts Uganda’s 64th Independence Anniversary After Landmark Trade Talks Facility for Talo and Leadership. Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. ABOUT US | PRIVACY POLICY Home Politics Security Elections More News Premium Economy & Business Education Entertainment & Lifestyle Environment & Climate Health International Relations Technology & Innovation Sports News By Region Nationwide Benadir Galmudug Hirshabelle Jubaland Puntland South West State Somaliland North Eastern State Resources Infographics Downloads Videos FAQ About Somalia",
      "summary": "Puntland President Said Abdullahi Deni has publicly rejected the federal election process proposed by President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, accusing him of obstructing dialogue and failing to honor agreed-upon electoral timelines. During the opening of Puntland's parliament session, Deni emphasized the need for urgent discussions among Somali stakeholders to prevent further political deterioration, asserting that his administration would not participate in the federal electoral process. This rejection reflects a broader discontent among Puntland and other regions, such as Jubaland, regarding the federal government's electoral timetable and constitutional amendments, which they claim centralize power excessively. The political tensions are compounded by recent violent incidents in Puntland, including the deaths of civilians, which have raised concerns about accountability and foreign military operations in the region. Deni's stance signals a significant rift between Puntland and the federal government, highlighting the fragility of Somalia's political landscape and the urgent need for a collaborative resolution.",
